.TH ZFETCH 1 "2026-11" "zfetch" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zfetch \- a small native Linux system summary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zfetch
[--json | --format text|json] [--no-color]
.SH DESCRIPTION
zfetch reads a bounded set of native system files and prints OS, kernel,
architecture, CPU, online logical CPU count, memory use, uptime, configured
shell and terminal identifier.
No package enumeration, network requests, external commands or resident process
are used. User names, host names, IP addresses and serial numbers are omitted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Emit one zfetch.system.v1 JSON object followed by a newline. All eleven keys
are present. Missing fields are null; bytes and seconds are integers. This
format never includes ANSI styling, terminal wrapping or a heading.
.TP
.B --format text|json
Select text or JSON output. --format=json is also accepted. The last format
option wins. Default redirected text retains its compact, uncolored layout.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI color. Redirected output, NO_COLOR (even when empty), and
TERM=dumb also disable color.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show help without probing the system.
.TP
.B -V, --version
Show the version without probing the system.
.SH TERMINAL LAYOUT
Interactive text groups system, hardware and session fields with whitespace.
Labels and values share rows at 40 columns or more; narrower terminals stack
them. Window width is read from stdout, with an 80-column fallback. UTF-8
wrapping conservatively reserves two cells per non-ASCII scalar. Wide glyphs
use a question mark in a one-column terminal. JSON retains the collected value.
.SH SOURCES
OS comes from os-release, kernel and architecture from uname, CPU from the first
4096 bytes of /proc/cpuinfo, and the CPU count from /sys/devices/system/cpu/online.
Memory use is MemTotal minus MemAvailable from /proc/meminfo. Uptime is read
from /proc/uptime. Shell is the basename of SHELL, not a claim about the parent
process. Terminal uses TERM_PROGRAM, falling back to TERM.
.SH LIMITS
Linux x86_64 and aarch64. Missing, inaccessible or unsupported fields display
unavailable; no network fallback is used. Output is one snapshot and can change
while the system is running. This utility intentionally does not reproduce
every fastfetch module.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 for success, including unavailable fields; 1 for output failure; 2 for an
invalid option; 101 for an internal failure. Diagnostics go to stderr. A pipe
closed by its reader normally terminates the producer with SIGPIPE; if SIGPIPE
was inherited as ignored, the write failure returns status 1.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proc (5),
.BR os-release (5)
